53-# attached to a check that does not know how to fix itself.
54-#
55-# Phase-092, 088 and 113 all failed the same two checks and burned three
56-# attempts each. The message said the rule and not the remedy, and a rule an
57-# agent cannot act on is not a gate - it is a wall. The retry note is supposed to
58-# close that gap, but a note is written by a human who has to notice, and three
59-# of these ran before anyone did.
60-#
61-# remedy_for prints the first line of advice for a message, keyed on a substring
62-# of it. It is a lookup rather than a per-check argument so that adding a check
63-# means adding a remedy rather than remembering to thread one through, and so
64-# the text lives in exactly one place and cannot drift from what it explains.
65-#
66-# When nothing matches, nothing is printed: a wrong remedy would send an agent
67-# chasing a problem it does not have, which is worse than the bare verdict it
68-# replaces.
69-remedy_for() {
70-  case "$1" in
71-    *"failure is produced"*)
72-      printf '          A suite of entirely passing tests does NOT satisfy this.\n'
73-      printf '          The check wants a test whose SUBJECT is a failure - a bad input\n'
74-      printf '          producing a clean error rather than a panic or a wrong value.\n'
75-      printf '          Rust:    let r = run_source(bad); assert!(r.is_err());\n'
