119-# =============================================================================
120-# RBOPS_PREFLIGHT=1 — the cheap half of the gate.
121-#
122-# Everything here is a question about a DIFF or a DOCUMENT. Nothing here needs a
123-# build, a test run, or an example to execute. So it can be asked BEFORE the
124-# agent is dispatched, against whatever the phase inherits (its recovery branch,
125-# its WIP checkpoint, or nothing yet).
126-#
127-# Why this exists, in numbers: phases 037 and 048 each burned three ~5-hour runs
128-# and 24 agent-hours total, and both were correct work refused by a DETECTOR —
129-# first `eval_err`, then `label()`, neither of which the failure-assert regex
130-# recognised. The agent cannot fix that; only the gate can. Asking the same
131-# questions seconds earlier, before the model call, turns a 5-hour failure into
132-# a 5-second one.
133-#
--
204:# --- 2. diff hygiene ---------------------------------------------------------
205:step "diff hygiene"
206-
207:# STAGE NEW FILES, then diff. `git diff <base>` compares the base tree to the
208-# working tree for TRACKED paths only — a brand-new file the agent wrote is
209-# untracked, and untracked files are invisible to it. Writing a new test file is
210-# the single most natural thing a phase does, and the gate was scoring those
211-# phases as if no test existed: phase-007 wrote 355 lines and 16 #[test]
212-# functions in tests/numeric_edge_test.rs and the gate reported "0 rust".
213-#
214-# `git add -N` (intent-to-add), deliberately NOT `git add -A`: it records the
215-# path in the index with no content, so the file becomes visible to the diff,
216-# while modifications to already-tracked files stay unstaged. That matters
217-# because `git checkout -- <path>` restores from the INDEX, so staging
218-# tracked edits would silently break every restore-from-HEAD in the pipeline.
219-git add -N . >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
220-
221:CHANGED="$(git diff --name-only "$BASE_REF" 2>/dev/null)"
222-if [ -z "$CHANGED" ]; then
223-  # An empty diff means two different things. Under the full gate it means the
224-  # phase changed nothing, which is a failure — and correctly so. Under
225-  # PREFLIGHT it usually means the phase is on its FIRST attempt and has not
226-  # written anything yet: there is nothing to judge, not something to reject.
227-  # Failing it there would defer every new phase before the agent ever ran.
228-  if [ "$PREFLIGHT" = "1" ]; then
229-    printf '\n\033[33m╔══════════════════════════════════════╗\033[0m\n'
230-    printf '\033[33m║ PREFLIGHT NOTHING TO JUDGE — %s ║\033[0m\n' "$PHASE"
231-    printf '\033[33m╚══════════════════════════════════════╝\033[0m\n'
232-    exit 4
233-  fi
234:  bad "no changed files detected against $BASE_REF"
235-fi
