403:# --- 5. test policy: edge cases and failure assertions are mandatory ---------
404:step "test policy"
405-# The quota is disjunctive, matching AGENTS.md 3.3: a phase qualifies with
406-# >=3 Rust tests OR >=2 Redblue tests. This is deliberately a low floor, not a
407-# target — it catches "no verification at all", and sufficiency beyond it is
408-# the reviewer's call. A high count once failed a good 5-test phase.
409-MIN_RS="$("$JQ" -r '.test_policy.min_rust_tests' "$PHASES" 2>/dev/null)" || MIN_RS=""
410-if [ -z "$MIN_RS" ]; then warn "could not read .test_policy.min_rust_tests; using 3"; MIN_RS=3; fi
411-MIN_RB="$("$JQ" -r '.test_policy.min_redblue_tests' "$PHASES" 2>/dev/null)" || MIN_RB=""
412-if [ -z "$MIN_RB" ]; then warn "could not read .test_policy.min_redblue_tests; using 2"; MIN_RB=2; fi
413-# Count the `#[test]` ATTRIBUTE, not a name prefix. Requiring `fn test_`/`fn
414-# edge_` meant a test named `modulo_by_zero_is_a_runtime_error` — perfectly
415-# clear, perfectly real — did not count toward the quota. phase-007 wrote 16
416-# #[test] functions and the quota read 0 because only the 6 edge_-prefixed ones
417-# matched. The prefix belongs to the edge_-naming rule below, which enforces it
418-# deliberately; the quota should be counting tests.
419-NEW_TESTS="$(git diff -U0 "$BASE_REF" -- '*.rs' 2>/dev/null | grep -cE '^\+\s*#\[test\]' )"
420-NEW_RB="$(git diff -U0 "$BASE_REF" -- '*.rb' 2>/dev/null | grep -cE '^\+\s*test ' )"
421-# Both idioms must be recognised, in BOTH target languages. The quota above is
422-# disjunctive (Rust OR Redblue), so these two rules have to be too — otherwise a
423-# Redblue-only phase can satisfy the quota and still be told it proved nothing.
424-#   Rust:    fn edge_empty_list()          Redblue:  test "edge_empty list"
425-# The optional quote is the whole bug: a Redblue test name is a string literal,
426-# so `test edge_x` never occurs, and the unquoted-only regex rejected 61 real
427-# tests in phase-003 including correctly-named ones.
428-EDGE="$(git diff -U0 "$BASE_REF" -- '*.rs' '*.rb' 2>/dev/null | grep -cE '^\+.*(fn |test +"?)edge_')"
429-# Some subsystems have no failure channel to assert on. A linter reports
430-# problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP produce documents,
431-# not errors. Requiring `is_err`/`should_panic` of them is a category error, and
432-# it is not hypothetical: phase-015 (formatter) and phase-016 (linter) were both
433-# blocked after burning every attempt on it, while phase-016's tests were
434-# asserting failure correctly as `assert_eq!(warnings.len(), 1, ...)`.
435-#
436-# Such a phase declares `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the check is
437-# SKIPPED, visibly — never silently passed, and never a global relaxation. The
438-# reviewer still judges whether the tests can actually fail, and every
439-# error-producing phase keeps the rule at full strength.
440-FAILASSERT_MODE="$("$JQ" -r --arg p "$PHASE" '.phases[] | select(.id==$p) | .failure_assert // "required"' "$PHASES" 2>/dev/null)" || FAILASSERT_MODE="required"
441-[ -n "$FAILASSERT_MODE" ] || FAILASSERT_MODE="required"
442-
443-# Failure assertions per language. Rust has is_err/expect_err/should_panic;
444-# Redblue has no exceptions and instead has `try ... catch error`, which was
445-# missing here entirely — the one construct Redblue offers for asserting a fault.
446-# Anchored to the line start so a test whose *data* mentions `catch error` cannot
447-# buy a pass.
448-#
449-# Rust also has the `eval_err` idiom: a helper that evaluates a program
450-# expecting an error, followed by `assert!(matches!(e, Error::...))`. Phase-037
451-# blocked a 1200-green phase on this rule with 12 such assertions in the diff
452-# and zero recognized strings — `let e = eval_err("random_choice([])");
453-# assert!(matches!(e, Error::Runtime(_, _)))` asserts a failure is produced as
454-# surely as `is_err()` does. Same class as the #[test]-attribute fix (007) and
455-# the bilingual rules: the detector was narrower than the language's real
456-# failure-asserting idioms. The reviewer remains the ceiling — a bare
457-# `let _ = eval_err(x);` with no assertion is gaming, and it reads as gaming.
458-if [ "$FAILASSERT_MODE" = "not_applicable" ]; then
459-  # Skipped, and said out loud. A silent pass here would be indistinguishable
460-  # from the rule being satisfied.
461-  warn "failure-assertion rule NOT APPLICABLE to this phase (failure_assert: not_applicable) — the reviewer still judges whether the tests can fail"
462-else
463-  # Redblue's Error carries a label rather than a Result, so a test that proves
464-  # a failure was produced reads `assert_eq!(label(&e), "RuntimeError")` through
465-  # a local helper rather than calling is_err() on a Result. Phase-048 blocked on
466-  # this with ~10 such assertions (every one documented as "failure asserted" in
467-  # its REPORT) and zero recognized strings. Detecting the assertion is what
468-  # matters, not which method spells it, so the pattern also accepts a helper
469-  # call beside a spelled-out error label: a test cannot buy this by naming a
